{
  "schema_version": "1.7.4",
  "published": "2026-08-05T05:13:47Z",
  "modified": "2026-08-05T05:13:47Z",
  "summary": "Pervasively obfuscated CLI package — manual review required",
  "details": "Package ships ~100 JavaScript files across commands/, functions/, utils/, and top-level modules that are uniformly obfuscated with hex-identifier mangling (`_0x......`) consistent with javascript-obfuscator output. File and directory naming hints at sender/mailer functionality (functions/snd/mailers/* with names like dnsml, ewsml, freml, locml, mxdml, mxsml, prxml, zmbvf, zmrml), DNS resolution (functions/jdg/dns-resolve.js, functions/vrf/dns-resolve.js), MX/SPF/DMARC checks (functions/deb/dmcd.js, functions/deb/spro.js), IP rotation/proxy handling (functions/ipr/rotator.js, functions/ipr/pxen.js), HTML→RTF/XLS conversion (functions/gtp/cHtmlRtf*.js, functions/gtp/cHtmlXls.js), credential/JWT handling (utils/jwtVerify.js, utils/tok.js, commands/auth.js), and a redirect scanner (functions/rdt/*). The combination — bulk email delivery primitives, MX/DNS probing, proxy/IP rotation, and uniform obfuscation across the entire codebase — is structurally consistent with bulk-mail / spam-tooling or phishing infrastructure, but the actual runtime destinations, data flows, and whether anything fires at install/import time were not confirmed in this analysis. No lifecycle hook content was traced, no hardcoded C2 endpoints were extracted, and no concrete installer-harm fingerprint (browser-credential enumeration, wallet extraction, env-var scraping to attacker host) was captured. Pervasive whole-codebase obfuscation in a CLI tool published to a public registry is itself a strong transparency concern — legitimate CLI utilities rarely obfuscate every file including type definitions, color helpers, and validation utilities. Routing to human review for de-obfuscation and behavioral analysis before a publish-feed verdict.",
